_masterdb=/var/opt/mssql/data/master.mdf

_setup_hint() {
	cat <<'MSG'

  Microsoft SQL Server 2025 is installed but not yet configured.

  1. Run the one-time setup (it asks for the edition, the licence terms and
     the "sa" password):

       sudo /opt/mssql/bin/mssql-conf setup

  2. Enable and start the service:

       sudo systemctl enable --now mssql-server

  3. Check it came up:

       systemctl status mssql-server
       sudo journalctl -u mssql-server -e

  The engine listens on TCP 1433 by default. Connect with sqlcmd, Azure Data
  Studio, DBeaver or any SQL Server client as user "sa".

MSG
}

post_install() {
	_setup_hint
}

post_upgrade() {
	# $1 = new version, $2 = old version
	local newmajor=${1%%.*} oldmajor=${2%%.*}

	if [ ! -f "$_masterdb" ]; then
		_setup_hint
		return 0
	fi

	[ "$newmajor" = "$oldmajor" ] && return 0

	cat <<MSG

  ############################################################################
  ##  SQL Server major version $oldmajor -> $newmajor : action required                ##
  ############################################################################

  The next time the engine starts it rewrites every database in
  /var/opt/mssql - master, msdb, model and all user databases - to the new
  on-disk format. This CANNOT BE UNDONE: version $oldmajor can no longer open
  those files afterwards, there is no downgrade path, and the two majors
  cannot run side by side because /opt/mssql and /var/opt/mssql are fixed
  locations.

  Database compatibility levels are preserved, so existing databases keep the
  behaviour they had; only the physical file format moves.

  NOTHING HAS BEEN CONVERTED YET. In keeping with Arch practice this package
  does not start, stop or restart services, so the decision and the timing are
  yours.

MSG

	if systemctl is-active --quiet mssql-server 2>/dev/null; then
		cat <<'MSG'
  The engine is still running from the previous version's binaries, which are
  held open on disk, so it is unaffected until you restart it. Take a backup
  while it can still be read by the old engine, then convert:

      sudo systemctl stop mssql-server
      sudo tar -C /var/opt --zstd -cf ~/mssql-pre-upgrade.tar.zst mssql
      sudo systemctl start mssql-server
      sudo journalctl -u mssql-server -f

MSG
	else
		cat <<'MSG'
  The service is stopped. Take a backup now if you want a way back, then
  start it to convert:

      sudo tar -C /var/opt --zstd -cf ~/mssql-pre-upgrade.tar.zst mssql
      sudo systemctl start mssql-server
      sudo journalctl -u mssql-server -f

  Note that it will also convert on the next boot if the unit is enabled.

MSG
	fi
}

post_remove() {
	cat <<'MSG'

  /var/opt/mssql has been left in place; it holds your databases. Remove it
  manually if you no longer need them:

      sudo rm -rf /var/opt/mssql

  If the engine was running it has not been stopped; this package does not
  manage service state. Stop any leftover process yourself.

MSG
}
